{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-128282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We differentiate the function, applying the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+24\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{(3-1)}-3\\cdot 2{x}^{(2-1)}+24\\cdot 1-7\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "176a1d1670dc87fc78fcfbd8eed27dca686dccf8374febd805c64d96e5b227bf"} {"question" "q-128282" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128282" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3x^2-6x+24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nPreviously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}-6x+24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ \n\n* Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x={x}_{1}=-4$ and $x={x}_{2}=2.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-4\\cdot 2=-8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128283" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "176a1d1670dc87fc78fcfbd8eed27dca686dccf8374febd805c64d96e5b227bf"} {"question" "q-128283" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128283" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ \n\nPreviously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}-6x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}-6x+24>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n-3(x+4)(x-2)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=-3(x+4)(x-2)$ has roots at $x=-4$ and $x=2,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/assets/41fbc51820e147872fddfd0f124f0235a26513992f07a4b5681f2d8cb25d4a3b.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is increasing for $x\\in (-4,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "176a1d1670dc87fc78fcfbd8eed27dca686dccf8374febd805c64d96e5b227bf"} {"question" "q-128284" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128284" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128284" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128284" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFor which values of $x$ does the function $f(x)$ have local maxima and local minima?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local minimum\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local maximum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have the derivative \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24=-3(x+4)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the two critical points $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ We use the first derivative test to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-5,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =-3(-5+4)(-5-2)=-21<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-3(0+4)(0-2)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-3(3+4)(3-2)=-21<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $-4$ | $(-4,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\n \nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n* At $x=-4,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-4$ is a local minimum.\n\n* At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128285" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "176a1d1670dc87fc78fcfbd8eed27dca686dccf8374febd805c64d96e5b227bf"} {"question" "q-128285" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128285" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128285" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128285" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFor which values of $x$ does the function $f(x)$ have local maxima and local minima?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local minimum\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local maximum\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhat are the global maximum and global minimum values of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,3]?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global maximum is ${f}_{max}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global minimum is ${f}_{min}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/state.json" "file_sha256" "a6f1d170e5a48ef81274ae586a7f78a7b3db9b5f3294e2c081b4dede1ce9ba71"} {"question" "q-128286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We already know the critical points of the function:\n\n$$\nx=-4,\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSince $x=-4$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, the critical points on the interval $[-1,3]$ are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=-1$ and $x=3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{2}+24(-1)-7=-33 \\\\ f(2) & =-(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}+24(2)-7=21 \\\\ f(3) & =-(3)^{3}-3(3)^{2}+24(3)-7=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step 2.\n\n* The greatest value is $f(2)=21,$ so the global maximum is ${f}_{max}=21.$\n\n* The smallest value is $f(-1)=-33,$ so the global min